2. Constructors
Constructors are used to initialize objects.
- Default constructor: Creates an object with default values.
- Parameterized constructor: Initializes an object with user-provided values.
- Overloaded constructor: Allows multiple ways of creating objects using different parameters.
Example: ParkingTicket Class

class ParkingTicket {

    int ticketNo;
    String vehicleNo;
    double fee;

    // Default constructor
    ParkingTicket() {
        this(0, "Unknown", 0.0);
    }

    // Parameterized constructor
    ParkingTicket(int ticketNo, String vehicleNo) {
        this(ticketNo, vehicleNo, 0.0);
    }

    // Overloaded constructor
    ParkingTicket(int ticketNo, String vehicleNo, double fee) {
        this.ticketNo = ticketNo;
        this.vehicleNo = vehicleNo;
        this.fee = fee;
    }

    void displayTicket() {
        System.out.println(ticketNo + " " + vehicleNo + " " + fee);
    }
}



3. Use of this Keyword and Constructor Chaining
- this refers to the current object.
- this.ticketNo refers to the instance variable.
- this() calls another constructor in the same class.
- Constructor chaining avoids repeated initialization code.
Example:
ParkingTicket() {
    this(0, "Unknown", 0.0);
}


4. Copy Constructor
A copy constructor creates a new object using the values of an existing object.
ParkingTicket(ParkingTicket p) {
    this.ticketNo = p.ticketNo;
    this.vehicleNo = p.vehicleNo;
    this.fee = p.fee;
}


It can be used to create a duplicate ticket record for reference. However, a duplicate ticket must not receive the same unique ticket number in an actual parking system. A new ticket number should be generated.
5. Object Creation – Stack and Heap Memory
ParkingTicket t1 = new ParkingTicket(101, "TN01AB1111", 50.0);
- t1 is a reference variable stored in the stack frame.
- The actual ParkingTicket object is created in heap memory.
- The reference variable points to the object.
- Multiple reference variables can refer to the same object.

